&lt;p&gt;Adjust to change&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Take responsibility for personal and professional behaviours and decisions&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Develop and maintain effective working relationships&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Communicate effectively including the use of empathy and sympathy where appropriate&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td width=&quot;226&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Responsibility&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td width=&quot;531&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Maintain a professional appearance and follow Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) protocols&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Take responsibility for own development&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Apply knowledge and skills effectively&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Prioritise and plan work&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td width=&quot;226&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Self-improvement&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td width=&quot;531&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Commit to learning and professional development&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Identify and implement measures to improve performance and competence&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Accept and act on constructive advice and guidance, seeking clarification to recognise own limitations&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Duration&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Typically 30 – 36 months&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Qualifications&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;On successful completion of the End Point Assessment the learner will be awarded the RCVS – accredited Licence to Practise Veterinary Nursing qualification at Level 3&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Link to professional registration &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Students achieving this apprenticeship standard will be eligible to apply for professional registration with the RCVS&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Level&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;3&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Review date&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After 3 years&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

occupationalSummary:
&lt;p&gt;This occupation is found in veterinary practices, which can include primary care, referral, private, corporate-owned, and animal charities. These practices provide essential care for sick and injured animals.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Additionally, some Registered Veterinary Nurses work in settings, such as emergency care centres or referral hospitals.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rescue centres, wildlife organisations and animal charities also employ Registered Veterinary Nurses, contributing to the welfare of animals in need.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Registered Veterinary Nurses can work in a range of settings ranging from independently owned practices through to large group organisations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They may progress into non-clinical roles in education, research and pharmaceutical settings and business support roles.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Registered Veterinary Nurses can work with a wide range of animals, from domestic pets to exotic species, wildlife, equine and livestock.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The broad purpose of the occupation is to provide essential care for animals.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A Registered Veterinary Nurse works as a member of the veterinary team, providing expert nursing care in line with the Veterinary Surgeons Act to ensure animal health and welfare.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Registered Veterinary Nurses perform hands-on nursing care, administer treatments, and assist with surgical procedures. They carry out technical work and are skilled in undertaking a range of diagnostic tests, monitoring anaesthesia, medical treatments and minor surgical procedures.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They also play a significant role educating animal owners on animal health and build strong client relationships.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Depending on the practice, they may focus on areas like emergency care or referrals.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Their working hours can include weekends and unsociable hours due to the 24/7 nature of animal care.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A Registered Veterinary Nurse interacts with internal and external stakeholders including animal owners.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They prioritise the delivery of animal health and welfare as part of a multi-disciplinary team.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Registered Veterinary Nurses collaborate with all members of the veterinary team as well as paraprofessionals.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Typical external stakeholders can include suppliers, other veterinary practices and charities.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The level of interaction varies based on the practice size and role within the organisation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A Registered Veterinary Nurse will be responsible for practising in accordance with the Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons (RCVS) Code of Professional Conduct for veterinary nurses.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Registered Veterinary Nurses are registered professionals and work within the standards and scope of practice defined by the RCVS http://www.rcvs.org.uk/about-us/the-role-of-the-rcvs/&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Registered Veterinary Nurses work with moderate autonomy, displaying leadership qualities and completing tasks independently whilst working within legal and ethical limits. They may manage, support and develop team members along with maintaining their own health and wellbeing, professional development, accountability and fitness to practice.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They are responsible for patient safety and apply evidence based techniques to implement clinical governance and quality improvement processes.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They are responsible for working safely, considering the environment, sustainability, and providing contextualised care.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They are required to comply with legislation relevant to the industry.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
